"## Verdict: **conditional accept**\n\nFour of the initial review's open items are now fixed by new evidence and the current source. Three are accepted with limits. The package-identity join is still unknown. I recommend accepting the guard, recovery, footprint and publication claims within the limits below. The candidate should not count as qualified until someone hashes the staged package and gets the frozen digest `df53c9a2…`.\n\nI made no audit submission and wrote no audit-result file, because writes and mutations were not allowed.\n\n## The two probes I ran\n- `inspect --ready` returned graph version 1, context `CTX-4F617FC1…`, and one ready task, `RESEARCH-101`, with guards `GUARD-TASK-A1B0…` and `GUARD-AUDIT-DB7C…`. This matches what the claude-candidate-final trace recorded.\n- `validate` returned `valid: true` with no errors at the same context.\n- This is the owned fixture, not the development plan.\n\n## Decision on each open item\n\n**1. Stale run refused after a changed input — accepted, with limits.**\n- `recovery-readable.json` now records a real 4.1 `audit --result pass` that exited with code 2: `Invalid harness proof: EVREQ-101-01: candidate inputs changed; collect fresh proof`. Readiness had reported the same blocker just before.\n- This replaces the summary flag the initial review objected to.\n- **Limits:**\n  - The step that changed the input, and the step that undid it, are not in the `cli` list. The trace only shows readiness flipping from not-ready back to ready.\n  - \"Canonical state unchanged after refusal\" has no before/after hashes here. The file points to `recovery-qualified-result.json` for full output, but that file is not in the evidence set.\n\n**2. Snapshot recovery and history retention — accepted, with limits.**\n- **Restore and history:**\n  - The restored archive is named: `PLAN-001-R1-G5-20261004T091451Z`.\n  - Each history record has before/after hashes, and they are identical.\n  - `C2` is not in the snapshot but survives the restore, which is what \"retain newer history\" claims.\n- **Proof and 4.0 behaviour:**\n  - The restored 4.0 proof (`RUN-FCB6…`, contract `a584…`) is byte-identical to the original.\n  - After the restore, 4.0 `validate` and 4.0 `--audit-readiness` both report ready.\n  - Before the restore, 4.0 validated the 4.1-bound state and said it was valid. Its readiness check then blocked it with \"proof contract changed\". That is the fail-safe disagreement the addendum describes, not mixed-version support. The README now says so (lines 36–39).\n- **Limit:** `snapshot_file_hashes` are hashes of the archive contents. No hashes of the live files after restore are compared against them.\n\n**3. Fresh guards versus the reviewed caller — accepted.**\n- `guard.json` records both guards that the frozen caller was invoked with:\n  - With the old take guard `…9891…`, it exited 2 with a scoped \"Stale task guard\" error.\n  - With the guard from the resume packet `…97AE…`, the release succeeded with reason \"Owned stable-caller qualification release\".\n- The caller hash `95510cfb…` is the same before and after.\n- `guard-caller.py` takes the guard as `sys.argv` and contains no guard literal.\n- **Limit:** I could not hash `evidence/guard-caller.py` to confirm it is the `95510cfb…` file, because no helper tools were allowed.\n\n**4. Footprint scan closing directory handles — accepted.**\n- `package/scripts/pyramid_assurance.py:150-152` closes every scan iterator still open in a `finally` block. Iterators are also closed when they are exhausted or hit an error (lines 101 and 106).\n- The README (lines 36–38) now says that a project query may create the lock file and that \"read-only\" means no change to plan state or proof.\n\n**5. Projection publishing — accepted, within the stated scope.**\n- `pyramid_core.py:2241-2243` writes `graph.json` last.\n- `test_runtime.py:1253` makes the README write fail and asserts that the graph bytes are unchanged. The test is meaningful because the graph includes `generated_at` (line 1932). Writing the graph early would therefore change its bytes.\n- `_publish_head` (line 636) hashes the plan and state files and is separate from these regenerated views.\n- **Limits (already disclosed):**\n  - Each file is replaced atomically, but there is no `fsync` and no atomic update across several views.\n  - The history index is rebuilt before any views are written (line 2112).\n- I read this test; I did not run it.\n\n**6. Decision text from the six cases — accepted, as decisions only.**\n- `native-decisions.md` has the complete text from all four hosts.\n- Both final hosts reach the expected decisions on all six cases, with explicit limits. For EXTENSION, they keep the old proof only through an explicit binding and require fresh proof when the source changes. For GUARD, they pass the guard at run time and re-review the caller if it changed.\n- Both baselines have defects:\n  - claude-baseline says the provider's proof stays current under 4.0. The real 4.0 trace contradicts this (\"proof contract changed\").\n  - codex-baseline offers editing the guard literal into the source as an option.\n- These are reasoned answers to supplied facts, not product runs.\n\n**7. Test log tied to the package and command — partially accepted.**\n- `test-result.json` binds:\n  - the exact command;\n  - package digest `df53c9a2…`;\n  - start and finish times (09:12:57–09:14:04 UTC), exit code 0, and \"176 passed; 17 skills / 31 schemas\";\n  - artifact hashes.\n- **Limits:**\n  - This is the coordinator's recorded run, not a run I did.\n  - I could not check that `tests.txt` hashes to `1c9a63bf…`.\n  - The `HTTPError 421` ResourceWarning is still in the log.\n  - The guard experiment's timestamps (09:14:03–04) fall inside the test window, so the two were run together or overlapped. That needs no fix but should be noted.\n\n## Still unknown or not resolved\n\n- **Package identity:** `package-identity.json` shows the source and the codex-candidate-final package both at `df53c9a2…`. Nothing hashes `focused-review/package/`, so I can't confirm that the source I read is the qualified candidate. The probe context matching claude-candidate-final is weak supporting evidence, not proof.\n- **`producer-4.0.json`:** it still has no runtime labels, so the `2a0e…` vs `b722…` contract-hash mismatch is unexplained inside that file. The addendum explains it as a formula-only experiment run on 4.1, but the file doesn't record that. Don't cite this file as 4.0 execution evidence. The real 4.0 evidence is now in `recovery-readable.json`, where the contract hash `a584…` is consistent end to end.\n- **Producer proof reused for the consumer:** a plan may let the producer's proof count toward the consumer's criterion. That is a plan-level choice the runtime can't judge. `TASK-202` and `GATE-290` stay unverified until they have their own composition proof.\n- **File names:** the addendum names files (`recovery-detailed-result.json`, `guard-qualified-result.json`, `qualified-test-*.json`, `consumer-growth-4.0.json`) that aren't in `evidence/`. The projections I reviewed stand in for them. Any claim that rests only on those full files is unverified.\n\n## What would close this\n1. Add a digest of `focused-review/package/` computed with the same algorithm.\n2. Label each call's runtime in `producer-4.0.json`, or retire it.\n3. Add the step that changed the input, the step that undid it, and the canonical hashes around the refused audit to the recovery projection.\n\nOut of scope and not claimed: power-loss durability, mixed-version support, an installed release, real product, browser or mail behaviour, and token savings."
